This is a set of cross-platform command line tools for working with MPEG data.

The emphasis is on relatively simple tools which concentrate on MPEG (H.264 and
H.262) data packaged according to H.222 (i.e., TS or PS), with a particular
interest in checking for conformance.

Transport Stream (TS) is typically used for distribution of cable and satellite
data. Program Stream (PS) is typically used to store data on DVDs.

The tools are focused on:

* Quick reporting of useful data (tsinfo, stream_type)
* Giving a quick overview of the entities in the stream (esdots, psdots)
* Reporting on TS packets (tsreport) or ES units/frames/fields (esreport)
* Simple manipulation of stream data (es2ts, esfilter, esreverse, esmerge, ts2es)
* Streaming of data, possibly with introduced errors (tsplay)

Building
--------

Build with GNU make:

    make
    sudo make install

The tools are installed into $(prefix)/bin (default /usr/local/bin) and the
man pages into /usr/local/man/man1. Override the destinations with, for
example, 'make install prefix=$HOME/.local bindir=$HOME/.local/bin
mandir=$HOME/.local/man' to install without root privileges.

macOS
-----

This fork is maintained for Apple Silicon (arm64 / ARM) macOS, and is the
fork to build from source on an ARM Mac. It is verified on arm64 /
macOS 27.x with the Xcode toolchain.

Note that Apple's libtool no longer builds dynamic libraries: 'libtool
-dynamic' was removed in recent releases, so libtstools.dylib is built with
'clang -dynamiclib' instead. The command line tools link the static library
(libtstools.a), which libtool -static still builds as before.

The tools are built as native arm64 binaries. On an Apple Silicon Mac they
run natively; no Rosetta translation is required.

Changes in this fork
--------------------

### ts2es: pass unwrapped AAC (ADTS) through unchanged (2026-11-12)

Upstream ts2es assumes that every elementary stream in a transport
stream is PES-wrapped: at each payload-unit start it reads
payload[4..5] as the PES packet length and runs any non-H.222 payload
through the MPEG-1 PES header heuristics (calc_mpeg1_pes_offset()).
For unwrapped AAC (ADTS, stream type 0x0F, ISO/IEC 13818-7) - a
compliant and common way of carrying AAC in a TS - that logic mangles
every frame: the ADTS sync (0xFFF) falls into the "MPEG-1 PES"
branch, which drops header bytes from each frame ("### MPEG-1 PES
packet has 0xfX instead of..." errors) and clamps continuation
packets against the bogus "PES length", so the extracted ES is
shorter than the real payload and will not decode.

ts2es now detects the ADTS sync at the start of a payload unit
(0xFF, byte 1 in 0xF0-0xF3 with the layer bits '00') and passes the
data through raw, without any PES-length clamping. PES-wrapped
streams (video, MPEG-1 audio, ...) are handled exactly as before;
output on such content is byte-identical to upstream.

### tsinfo: TDT/TM detection, validation and reporting (2026-11-12)

Upstream tsinfo parses only PAT and PMT: it never looks at TDT/TM
(system time), so a corrupt TDT - for example a month byte of 0xbb
that is not valid BCD - passed through silently.

tsinfo now sniffs PUSI payloads for TDT (0x13) and TM (0x14)
sections on any PID (their PIDs are not known in advance, so this
works without SDT parsing), and for each distinct section it:

- checks the section size (section_length 16, 19 bytes total);
- checks the section CRC-32 (init 0xFFFFFFFF, the same convention as
  the PAT/PMT checks);
- validates the BCD date/time fields (year, month, day, hour,
  minute, second), accepting the EN 300 468 binary encodings of days
  26-31 (0x1A-0x1F), and range-checks them (month 1-12, day 1-31,
  hour 0-23, minute/second 0-59);
- reports the decoded time: UTC for TDT, local time for TM.

Findings are reported in the usual ### / !!! style, once per
distinct section, so a table repeating every few packets does not
flood the output. Muxes without TDT/TM produce exactly the same
output as upstream.

### Known limitations (unchanged from upstream)

- tsinfo still does not parse EIT at all: an invalid EIT (or any
  other table's content) produces no tsinfo warning. TDT/TM are now
  covered (see above).
- ts2es still assumes PES wrapping for video and other non-ADTS
  streams: raw Annex B H.264 carried without PES in a TS is not
  extracted faithfully (the MPEG-1 PES heuristics may drop bytes).
  Raw MP2/MP3 audio is affected in the same way.
